WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:02.100
Instructor: So just like with branches,

00:02.100 --> 00:04.590
we can actually check out particular tags.

00:04.590 --> 00:05.760
The main difference, though,

00:05.760 --> 00:07.418
is that when we're checking out a tag,

00:07.418 --> 00:12.229
a tag is referring to a a particular commit, not a branch.

00:12.229 --> 00:13.912
There's a a big difference there,

00:13.912 --> 00:17.040
where a branch reference is always going to be updated

00:17.040 --> 00:19.620
to refer to the tip of that branch.

00:19.620 --> 00:20.670
We check out that branch.

00:20.670 --> 00:22.260
We're not in detached head.

00:22.260 --> 00:24.926
But if we instead check out a particular tag,

00:24.926 --> 00:27.239
we are going to go into detached head,

00:27.239 --> 00:29.753
because we're basically just checking out a commit,

00:29.753 --> 00:33.090
just like if we actually check out a particular commit

00:33.090 --> 00:38.090
like git checkout 532FB, whatever that commit hash is,

00:38.257 --> 00:40.261
that is gonna put us into detached head.

00:40.261 --> 00:42.609
Same thing when we check out a tag,

00:42.609 --> 00:45.763
because a tag is just pointing to a commit.

00:45.763 --> 00:47.198
So it's not a problem.

00:47.198 --> 00:48.420
If we want to go back

00:48.420 --> 00:51.090
and actually work on an older version...

00:51.090 --> 00:54.450
Usually, you don't just make changes to an old release.

00:54.450 --> 00:57.090
The point is that a release is set in stone.

00:57.090 --> 00:59.400
When it's released, it doesn't change.

00:59.400 --> 01:00.870
That's what new releases are for

01:00.870 --> 01:02.250
if you need to make changes.

01:02.250 --> 01:04.735
But let's say we wanted to go back and work on

01:04.735 --> 01:08.190
or work off of some earlier release.

01:08.190 --> 01:12.510
How about, sure, 15.3.1.

01:12.510 --> 01:13.530
All right?

01:13.530 --> 01:18.030
All that we have to do is git checkout that right there.

01:18.030 --> 01:19.446
15.3.1.

01:19.446 --> 01:21.165
So that's a tag name.

01:21.165 --> 01:23.940
If I try and use checkout normally,

01:23.940 --> 01:26.179
we check out a branch or we check out a commit hash.

01:26.179 --> 01:28.230
That's not exactly what one looks like.

01:28.230 --> 01:30.794
Or we can check out a tag.

01:30.794 --> 01:33.210
And we see our favorite message,

01:33.210 --> 01:35.040
that we're now in detached head state.

01:35.040 --> 01:37.582
You can look around, make experimental changes, commit them,

01:37.582 --> 01:40.952
and we can make a branch if we want to keep working.

01:40.952 --> 01:42.516
So I could do that.

01:42.516 --> 01:46.500
I could do a git switch -c, MY,

01:46.500 --> 01:50.740
I don't know what I wanna call this, BRANCH_FROM_TAG.

01:51.600 --> 01:52.890
I don't know why I'm using underscores here.

01:52.890 --> 01:54.180
Normally, I wouldn't do that.

01:54.180 --> 01:56.283
But anyway, I now made this branch

01:56.283 --> 02:01.283
based upon that particular commit, which was 15.3.1.

02:01.290 --> 02:02.830
That's what the tag referred to.

02:02.830 --> 02:06.150
And we probably won't be able to tell much difference

02:06.150 --> 02:07.740
here at all.

02:07.740 --> 02:09.511
But that brings me to the next point,

02:09.511 --> 02:12.467
which is that we can actually diff different tags.

02:12.467 --> 02:15.311
So I think a more obvious diff to do

02:15.311 --> 02:18.082
would be between two patches.

02:18.082 --> 02:19.920
So what about this?

02:19.920 --> 02:22.576
17.0.0, so that was a major release,

02:22.576 --> 02:24.600
and then there was a patch.

02:24.600 --> 02:27.240
So I want to see what changed between these two.

02:27.240 --> 02:28.980
I can do that very easily.

02:28.980 --> 02:33.980
So I can do git diff and then v17., if I can type it, .0.0,

02:35.910 --> 02:40.910
and then I want v17.0.1, like that.

02:41.637 --> 02:45.780
And it looks like it was a very minor change.

02:45.780 --> 02:47.130
This is the only thing that changed,

02:47.130 --> 02:51.060
one file and just a little bit of what,

02:51.060 --> 02:53.470
a couple of lines added and one line removed.

02:53.470 --> 02:55.170
So that was it.

02:55.170 --> 02:58.468
But if we were to compare something much more drastic,

02:58.468 --> 03:02.833
how about the last minor release on 16?

03:03.714 --> 03:04.860
So 16.14.

03:04.860 --> 03:07.620
So let's just find that, 16.14.

03:07.620 --> 03:09.870
It probably starts with a V.

03:09.870 --> 03:10.713
Is it on here?

03:12.570 --> 03:13.403
Yes. Okay.

03:13.403 --> 03:17.145
16.14.0. And compare that to 17.

03:17.145 --> 03:22.145
So git diff 16.14.0 and we'll compare that to v17.0.0.

03:23.336 --> 03:27.030
So this was the last minor on 16

03:27.030 --> 03:31.067
and the first major or a new major release v17.

03:31.067 --> 03:33.917
And I'm gonna be scrolling for a while.

03:33.917 --> 03:36.360
There are lots of files, lots of changes,

03:36.360 --> 03:38.032
lots of stuff deleted.

03:38.032 --> 03:40.759
I don't even know how much.

03:40.759 --> 03:42.270
It doesn't gimme an indication

03:42.270 --> 03:44.370
of how much more I have to go, but lots of changes.

03:44.370 --> 03:45.780
It doesn't help that I'm very zoomed in

03:45.780 --> 03:47.160
to make the text large.

03:47.160 --> 03:49.470
But anyway, those are two points in time.

03:49.470 --> 03:50.970
They are marked with tags.

03:50.970 --> 03:53.070
We can check them out and make a new branch

03:53.070 --> 03:55.668
if we wanna work based upon one of those points in time,

03:55.668 --> 03:57.482
or we can compare them.

03:57.482 --> 03:59.790
These are just moments in time.

03:59.790 --> 04:01.800
They're releases in this case,

04:01.800 --> 04:04.236
but as we'll soon see, we can create tags with any name.

04:04.236 --> 04:05.910
We'll stick with releases.

04:05.910 --> 04:06.990
But they're just a pointer.

04:06.990 --> 04:08.640
It's just like a sticky note on a commit

04:08.640 --> 04:11.223
that says here is 17.0.0,

04:11.223 --> 04:13.290
so everyone in the future knows

04:13.290 --> 04:15.330
that this is where that release was.

04:15.330 --> 04:17.340
This is what we released right here.

04:17.340 --> 04:18.870
So that's how we can check out tags

04:18.870 --> 04:20.430
and how we can diff them.

04:20.430 --> 04:22.530
The next thing, of course, is making tags.
